✅ 32. Alphabet–Number Pattern — Two Threads
🎗️ 32.1 Problem Statement
Implement a multithreaded program where two threads cooperate to print:
A1B2C3...
For:
repetitions = 3
Expected output:
A1B2C3
👉 One thread prints alphabets:
A B C
👉 Another thread prints numbers:
1 2 3
👉 The output must alternate:
A1B2C3
🎗️ 32.2 Your Design
Your implementation uses one shared object:
AlphabetNumber alphabetNumber = new AlphabetNumber(3);
Both threads use this same object.
                 AlphabetNumber
                /              \
               /                \
     Alphabet Thread        Number Thread
            |                     |
    printAlphabet()         printNumber()
Therefore, both threads synchronize on the same monitor.
🎗️ 32.3 Main Components
Your AlphabetNumber class contains:
private boolean turn;
private int number;
private int repetitions;
Each variable has a different responsibility.
Variable	Purpose
turn	Determines whose turn it is
number	Current number being printed
repetitions	Maximum number to print


Initially:
turn = true;
number = 1;
repetitions = 3;
So the initial state is:
turn = true
number = 1
🎗️ 32.4 Meaning of turn
In your implementation:
private boolean turn;
You are using:
turn = true
      ↓
Alphabet Thread's turn

turn = false
      ↓
Number Thread's turn
Therefore:
true  → Alphabet
false → Number
This is the synchronization state.
🎗️ 32.5 Why number Is Shared in Your Design
This is an important difference from the previous design.
Your code has:
private int number;
inside the shared AlphabetNumber object.
Both methods use it.
Alphabet Thread uses:
char ch = (char)(number - 1 + 'A');
Number Thread uses:
System.out.print(number);
number = number + 1;
Therefore:
number
  ↑
  │
  ├── Alphabet Thread reads it
  │
  └── Number Thread reads + modifies it
So number is genuinely shared state in your implementation.
🎗️ 32.6 How Alphabet Is Generated
You don't maintain:
char current = 'A';
Instead, you derive the alphabet from number.
Your expression is:
char ch = (char)(number - 1 + 'A');
Let's understand it.
When:
number = 1
we calculate:
1 - 1 + 'A'
= 0 + 'A'
= 'A'
When:
number = 2
we calculate:
2 - 1 + 'A'
= 1 + 'A'
= 'B'
When:
number = 3
we calculate:
3 - 1 + 'A'
= 2 + 'A'
= 'C'
Therefore:
number = 1 → A
number = 2 → B
number = 3 → C
