🎗️ 32.23 Why synchronized?
Your methods are:
public synchronized void printAlphabet()
and:
public synchronized void printNumber()
Because both are synchronized instance methods, both synchronize on:
this
which is the same AlphabetNumber object.
So:
AlphabetNumber
       ↓
     Monitor
      /   \
     /     \
Alphabet   Number
Thread     Thread
Only one thread can execute synchronized code on this object at a time.
🎗️ 32.24 Why Same AlphabetNumber Object?
Your main method creates:
AlphabetNumber alphabetNumber =
        new AlphabetNumber(3);
Then both threads receive that same object:
new Thread(() -> {
    alphabetNumber.printAlphabet();
});
and:
new Thread(() -> {
    alphabetNumber.printNumber();
});
Therefore:
             alphabetNumber
                   |
             Shared Object
              /         \
             /           \
Alphabet Thread       Number Thread
This is essential.
If you created two objects:
AlphabetNumber-1
       ↓
Alphabet Thread

AlphabetNumber-2
       ↓
Number Thread
then:
turn
number
monitor
would all be different.
The threads would not coordinate correctly.
🎗️ 32.25 notify() vs notifyAll() in Your Code
Your implementation uses:
notify();
Because you have exactly:
1 Alphabet Thread
1 Number Thread
there is only one possible waiting counterpart.
So in this particular design:
notify();
can work.
However, the more general recommendation is:
notifyAll();
especially if you later extend the program to multiple Alphabet or Number threads.
With:
notifyAll();
all waiting threads wake and re-check their conditions.
🎗️ 32.26 Why while Is Important
You correctly use:
while(!turn){
    wait();
}
and:
while(turn){
    wait();
}
This is better than:
if(!turn){
    wait();
}
because waking up does not automatically mean:
"It is definitely my turn."

The thread must re-check the state.
Correct pattern:
wait()
  ↓
wake up
  ↓
check condition again
  ↓
condition true?
  ↓
NO → wait again
YES → continue
🎗️ 32.27 Your Extra Termination Check
You have:
if(number>repetitions){
    notify();
    break;
}
This is a defensive termination check.
Conceptually:
Thread wakes
    ↓
Check whether work is already finished
    ↓
YES
    ↓
notify()
    ↓
break
It protects against the situation where a thread wakes after the shared number has already exceeded the limit.
For your current equal-length problem, the outer:
while(number<=repetitions)
already handles normal termination.
But the additional check makes the termination intent explicit.
🎗️ 32.28 Important Difference From the Earlier Design
There are two valid ways to design this problem.
Design 1 — Thread-Owned Sequence State
Shared:
    turn

Alphabet Thread:
    current = 'A'

Number Thread:
    current = 1
Here the sequence values are local.
Design 2 — Your Current Design
Shared:
    turn
    number

Alphabet:
    derived from number

Number:
    prints and increments number
Your design:
char ch = (char)(number - 1 + 'A');
means you don't need a separate alphabet counter.
So your shared state is:
turn
number
rather than just:
turn
🎗️ 32.29 Is Sharing number Wrong?
No.
Your program is correctly synchronized.
The important point is:
number
is genuinely accessed by both threads.
Alphabet Thread:
char ch = (char)(number - 1 + 'A');
Number Thread:
System.out.print(number);
number++;
Since the methods are synchronized on the same object, access is coordinated.
So this is a valid solution.
🎗️ 32.30 Is It the Most Minimal Shared-State Design?
Not necessarily.
If we follow strict ownership:
Alphabet Thread
    ↓
current = 'A'

Number Thread
    ↓
current = 1

Coordinator
    ↓
turn
then only:
turn
needs to be shared.
Your approach instead uses:
turn
number
as shared state.
This is still correct, but it gives the shared object more responsibility.
🎗️ 32.31 Common Mistakes
❌ Creating separate AlphabetNumber objects
❌ Using if instead of while
❌ Calling wait() outside synchronized code
❌ Forgetting to change turn
❌ Incrementing number before printing
❌ Incrementing number in the Alphabet Thread
❌ Forgetting notify()
❌ Calling run() instead of start()
❌ Using busy waiting
❌ Synchronizing the two methods on different objects
🎗️ 32.32 Best Practices
✔ Use one shared AlphabetNumber object.
✔ Synchronize both methods on the same object.
✔ Use while around wait().
✔ Change turn after printing.
✔ Increment number after printing the number.
✔ Keep the synchronization logic inside AlphabetNumber.
✔ Use notifyAll() if the design may later support multiple waiting threads.
✔ Handle InterruptedException properly.
🎗️ 32.33 Advantages of Your Approach
✔ Simple implementation
✔ No separate alphabet counter
✔ Alphabet can be derived from number
✔ Easy to understand
✔ Only one shared sequence counter
✔ Demonstrates wait() / notify()
✔ Demonstrates monitor-based synchronization
✔ Produces deterministic alternating output
🎗️ 32.34 Disadvantages of Your Approach
❌ number is shared between the two threads.
❌ Alphabet Thread depends on the shared number.
❌ Less strict separation of thread-owned state.
❌ notify() is less flexible than notifyAll() for multiple waiting threads.
❌ Extending the solution to many sequences becomes more complicated.

🎗️ 32.35 wait() vs sleep()
| `wait()` | `sleep()` |
|---|---|
| Releases monitor | Does not release monitor |
| Used for communication | Used for delay |
| Must be called while owning monitor | Does not require monitor |
| Can be awakened through notification | Waits for timeout/interruption |
| Used for synchronization | Used for timing |


👉 Interview Answer
wait() releases the monitor and puts the thread into the waiting state, while sleep() pauses the thread without releasing a monitor it already owns.

🎗️ 32.36 Busy Waiting vs wait()
❌ Busy Waiting
while (!turn) {
}
The CPU keeps checking the condition.
✅ Correct
while (!turn) {
    wait();
}
The thread releases the monitor and waits efficiently.
Flow:
Condition false
      ↓
wait()
      ↓
Release monitor
      ↓
WAITING
      ↓
Other thread changes turn
      ↓
notify()
      ↓
Wake up
      ↓
Re-check condition
🎗️ 32.37 Interview Questions
Q1. What does turn = true mean?
Alphabet Thread's turn.
Q2. What does turn = false mean?
Number Thread's turn.
Q3. Why is number shared?
Because the Alphabet Thread reads it to derive the character and the Number Thread prints and increments it.
Q4. Why do both methods use synchronized?
Because both access shared state and must coordinate through the same monitor.
Q5. What object is used as the monitor?
The shared AlphabetNumber object, effectively this.
Q6. Why use while around wait()?
To re-check the condition after waking.
Q7. Why is the alphabet calculated using number?
Because:
1 → A
2 → B
3 → C
So:
(char)(number - 1 + 'A')
generates the corresponding alphabet.
Q8. Why increment number only in printNumber()?
Because the number represents the completed pair.
After:
A
we still need:
1
Only after printing 1 should we move to:
B2
Q9. Can notify() be used here?
Yes, because there are only two threads and only one counterpart can be waiting.
Q10. Would notifyAll() also work?
Yes, and it is generally more flexible if multiple threads are introduced.
